</figure>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Choice of alignment score.</span> Table <a href="#A3.T4.st1" title="In Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Additional experimental results ‣ Data Selection for Fine-tuning Vision Language Models via Cross Modal Alignment Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> compares different choices of the alignment score (Definition <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 4.1 (Cross-modal alignment score 𝜎). ‣ 4.1 Finding Clusters of Examples with Similar Gradient ‣ 4 Method: Extracting Non-redundant Subsets of the VIT Data ‣ Data Selection for Fine-tuning Vision Language Models via Cross Modal Alignment Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). For a single checkpoint, instead of summing the top-5 singular values, one option is to concatenate them into a 5-dimensional vector, resulting in an alignment trajectory of size 35 across 7 checkpoints. Another option is to use the singular vector (dimension 576) corresponding to the largest singular value, giving a trajectory of size 4032 in Equation <a href="#S4.E7" title="In Definition 4.2 (Alignment trajectory.). ‣ 4.1.2 Bounding Gradient Distance Throughout Finetuning ‣ 4.1 Finding Clusters of Examples with Similar Gradient ‣ 4 Method: Extracting Non-redundant Subsets of the VIT Data ‣ Data Selection for Fine-tuning Vision Language Models via Cross Modal Alignment Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. As shown, summing the top-5 singular values outperforms the alternatives by about 2%.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T5" class="ltx_table">

